  {
   "id": "concept2",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "2's complement and subtraction"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "The 2'*s complement* of a number is its 1's complement plus 1. In n bits it represents the negative: −x is stored as 2^n − x. There is a single zero, the sign is still the leftmost bit, and ordinary binary addition just works: A − B is computed as A + (2's complement of B), dropping any carry out of the leftmost bit. An n-bit 2's complement integer ranges from −2^(n−1) to 2^(n−1) − 1, which is why Java's byte holds −128 to 127."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "2'*s complement* = 1's complement + 1"
    },
    {
     "en": "One zero; sign bit on the left"
    },
    {
